17.text:
The ionization chamber directly measures absorbed power in air at the focus via \(P = I \times (W/e) \approx 6.79 \times 10^{-11}\) J/s (standard \(W/e = 33.97\) J/C). The irradiated air mass is obtained strictly from the focused beam cross-section (\(0.3\) mm \(\times 6\) mm), chamber length, and given density, yielding \(m \approx 3.515 \times 10^{-4}\) g. Division produces the dose rate \(\dot{D}_\text{air} \approx 1.933 \times 10^{-4}\) Gy/s. Because \(\mu_\text{en}/\rho\) is stated to be identical for tissue and air, the tissue dose rate is the same. The supplied scan factor already encodes the effective irradiation time per tissue point (0.02 s), so the cumulative surface dose is simply \(1.933 \times 10^{-4} \times 0.02 = 3.87\) μGy.

(The monochromator aperture, 33 keV value, and explicit \(\mu_\text{en}/\rho\) are superfluous once the measured current at focus is available. Apparent nGy or mGy results in other posts arise solely from decimal/unit slips in the dose-rate step; the fluence-based route of Colleague 16 is inconsistent with the direct ionization measurement.)

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3.87 μGy}

