18.text:
The ionization chamber current directly measures absorbed power in the traversed air mass (via the standard \(W/e=33.97\) J/C conversion) once the chamber is located at the focus. The irradiated air mass is obtained strictly from the focused beam cross-section (\(0.3\) mm \(\times6\) mm), chamber length, and given density. Division then yields the instantaneous absorbed-dose rate in air (identical to tissue by the stated \(\mu_\text{en}/\rho\) equivalence). The supplied scan ratio already encodes the effective irradiation time experienced by any tissue voxel, so the cumulative surface dose is simply the product of that rate and the 0.02 s factor.

All other supplied parameters are superfluous once the measured current at the focus is available; they serve only to justify the thin-cavity and air/tissue-equivalence assumptions. Apparent nGy/mGy results arise solely from unit-conversion slips; minority results that insert an extra monochromator-to-focus compression factor after already using the focus dimensions are inconsistent (they double-count the fluence).

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3.87 μGy}

